    {
      "prompt": "Why is cookie authentication alone not a complete CSRF defense?",
      "conversation_id": "conversation-c35f391c-072b-4563-af77-c0979c0b0001",
      "turn": {
        "student_message": {
          "id": "message-f53577af-597f-4810-8c72-0231f02ac570",
          "conversation_id": "conversation-c35f391c-072b-4563-af77-c0979c0b0001",
          "role": "student",
          "content": "Why is cookie authentication alone not a complete CSRF defense?",
          "action": "question",
          "trace": null,
          "client_request_id": "demo-seed-security-targeted-001",
          "response_to_message_id": null,
          "tutoring_mode": "governed-autonomous-tutoring-graph-v2.1",
          "tutoring_intent": null,
          "learner_state_revision": null,
          "created_at": "2026-09-09T12:16:30+00:00"
        },
        "tutor_message": {
          "id": "message-4b435d95-827d-4bf9-9c62-049e621971e6",
          "conversation_id": "conversation-c35f391c-072b-4563-af77-c0979c0b0001",
          "role": "tutor",
          "content": "Here is the relevant course explanation. Demo Browser Security: session controls\n\nSessions\n\nAfter authentication, a session cookie can identify a server-side session. The server must check session validity and authorization on protected requests. Logout revokes the session in this course's example application.\n\nCookie controls\n\nSecure restricts transmission to secure connections. HttpOnly prevents JavaScript from reading the cookie through document.cookie; it does not prevent every action an injected script could perform. SameSite controls cookie inclusion in cross-site request contexts.\n\nCSRF\n\nA browser can automatically attach cookies to requests. This is why cookie authentication alone is not a complete CSRF defense. In the course example, state-changing requests require a permitted Origin; token-based defenses are another approach outside this example's implementation scope.\n\nContrast question\n\nHttpOnly limits cookie access by script; it does not by itself prove that a state-changing request was intended by the user. Try one step yourself.",
          "action": "answer",
